      47.99,
      188.14,
      420.05,
      213.84
    ],
    "reading_order": 7,
    "text": "The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic has many applications, both within\nmathematics and in other fields. Let us look at some examples.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p004_b08",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      47.99,
      223.73,
      420.04,
      249.83
    ],
    "reading_order": 8,
    "text": "Example 1 : Consider the numbers 4n, where n is a natural number. Check whether\nthere is any value of n for which 4n ends with the digit zero.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p004_b09",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      254.92,
      420.08,
      320.63
    ],
    "reading_order": 9,
    "text": "Solution : If the number 4n, for any n, were to end with the digit zero, then it would be\ndivisible by 5. That is, the prime factorisation of 4n would contain the prime 5. This is\nnot possible because 4n = (2)2n; so the only prime in the factorisation of 4n is 2. So, the\nuniqueness of the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic guarantees that there are no\nother primes in the factorisation of 4n. So, there is no natural number n for which 4n",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p004_b10",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      321.57,
      152.9,
      333.83
    ],
    "reading_order": 10,
    "text": "ends with the digit zero.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p004_b11",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      338.85,
      420.06,
      390.71
    ],
    "reading_order": 11,
    "text": "You have already learnt how to find the HCF and LCM of two positive integers\nusing the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic in earlier classes, without realising it!\nThis method is also called the prime factorisation method. Let us recall this method\nthrough an example.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p004_b12",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      400.36,
      420.03,
      413.03
    ],
    "reading_order": 12,
    "text": "Example 2 : Find the LCM and HCF  of 6 and 20 by the prime factorisation method.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p004_b13",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      414.21,
      362.27,
      430.07
    ],
    "reading_order": 13,
    "text": "Solution : We have :\n6 = 21 × 31 and 20 = 2 × 2 × 5 = 22 × 51.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p004_b14",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      435.09,
      420.01,
      460.55
    ],
    "reading_order": 14,
    "text": "You can find HCF(6, 20) = 2 and LCM(6, 20) = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5 = 60, as done in your\nearlier classes.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p004_b15",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      465.15,
      420.0,
      477.83
    ],
    "reading_order": 15,
    "text": "Note that HCF(6, 20) = 21 = Product of the smallest power of each common",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 4,
